Milestone 4 E2E instance isolation

Reason
The full macOS journey exposed a production-boundary collision that unit tests missed: while the installed Beaver application was open, the E2E bundle shared com.clgo.dash, so the single-instance plugin exited the test app with code 0 before setup. Test profiles isolated data but not the process identity.

Changes
- Move com.clgo.dash.e2e into the shared E2E overlay so macOS, Linux and Windows all own a distinct application identity.
- Keep the Windows-only Beaver E2E product name in its NSIS overlay.
- Test that one shared authority owns the identifier and the Windows overlay does not duplicate it.

TDD evidence
- Before the config change, e2e-process.test.mjs failed exactly one assertion: expected com.clgo.dash.e2e, actual undefined.
- After the change, the combined E2E Node suite passed 27/27.

Native evidence before correction
- E2E_REQUIRE_CEF_SMOKE=1 npm run test:e2e built Beaver.app but the app exited before embedded WebDriver readiness with code 0 and lifecycle stopped after native-prepared.
- The installed /Applications/Beaver.app process was simultaneously alive, confirming the single-instance collision.

Remaining proof
The full macOS CEF journey must be rerun on this commit; Windows/Linux identity behaviour remains for CI.

Native proof after correction
- E2E_REQUIRE_CEF_SMOKE=1 npm run test:e2e: passed on macOS while the installed /Applications/Beaver.app remained open.
